1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,088
This is STAT 331, video 19. It follows Lecture 9: categorical covariates, and hypothesis testing.

2
00:00:09,538 --> 00:00:16,002
This video prepares you for four question types. Each type gets a method card and worked problems.

3
00:00:16,452 --> 00:00:24,473
Type A: write a regression model with indicator variables for a categorical covariate, and interpret its parameters.

4
00:00:24,923 --> 00:00:34,715
Type B: compare two groups, or estimate one mean. This is a t test or a confidence interval for one linear combination of the parameters.

5
00:00:35,165 --> 00:00:40,264
Type C: test whether all groups have the same mean. This is the F test.

6
00:00:40,714 --> 00:00:48,479
Type D: derive the distribution of the F statistic, and show that F equals t squared when q is 1.

7
00:00:48,929 --> 00:01:13,335
Section 1 puts a categorical covariate into a regression model. First, slides 3 to 7 recap the tools. The model is y equals X beta plus epsilon. The errors are multivariate normal with mean 0 and variance sigma squared times I. The least squares estimate is X transpose X inverse, times X transpose y.

8
00:01:13,785 --> 00:01:35,182
beta hat is normal with mean beta, and variance sigma squared times X transpose X inverse. So one estimate, beta j hat, is normal with mean beta j, and variance sigma squared times V j j. V j j is the diagonal entry j j of X transpose X inverse.

9
00:01:35,632 --> 00:01:49,883
Replace sigma by sigma hat. Then beta j hat minus beta j, over sigma hat times the square root of V j j, has a t distribution with n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom.

10
00:01:50,333 --> 00:02:05,671
From this t result, we get a confidence interval for beta j, and a test of beta j equal to theta 0. The p-value is the probability that the absolute value of T is at least the absolute observed t.

11
00:02:06,121 --> 00:02:17,407
The same steps give a confidence interval for the mean response, and a prediction interval for a new y. This video adds one new type of covariate, and one new test.

12
00:02:17,857 --> 00:02:29,697
Slide 9 asks for the meaning of beta j. Fix all covariates except x j. Then the mean of y is a straight line in x j. The green line shows it.

13
00:02:30,147 --> 00:02:46,403
Increase x j by one unit, from a to a plus 1. The mean of y changes by beta j, the purple step. So beta j is the difference in mean y for a one-unit increase in x j, with all other covariates fixed.

14
00:02:46,853 --> 00:03:02,469
Fix the other covariates at different values. The line moves, but its slope stays beta j. So this interpretation needs a covariate where one unit has a meaning. The next slides show a covariate where this is not true.

15
00:03:02,919 --> 00:03:20,817
Here is the data example of Lecture 9. The file fishermen mercury dot c s v has 135 villagers from Kuwait. The outcome is MeHg: the concentration of methyl mercury in a hair sample, in milligrams per gram.

16
00:03:21,267 --> 00:03:44,179
One covariate is fishpart: the parts of fish that a subject eats. Code 0 is none, N. Code 1 is muscle tissue only, M. Code 2 is muscle tissue and sometimes the whole fish, MW. Code 3 is the whole fish, W. The table shows the first rows from the slide. We also use weight, in kilograms.

17
00:03:44,629 --> 00:04:00,693
fishpart is a categorical covariate. A categorical covariate puts each subject into one of a few categories, or levels. The plot shows MeHg for each level. The group sizes are 10, 28, 88, and 9.

18
00:04:01,143 --> 00:04:12,215
The purple marks are the group means. Group N has a much lower mean than the other three groups. The next slides show how to put such a covariate into a regression model.

19
00:04:12,665 --> 00:04:24,441
The first idea is to use the code as a number. The model is MeHg i equals beta 0, plus beta 1 times fishpart i, plus epsilon i.

20
00:04:24,891 --> 00:04:34,555
Then the mean of MeHg is a straight line in the code. The four groups sit on this green line, at the codes 0, 1, 2, and 3.

21
00:04:35,005 --> 00:04:56,253
beta 1 is the mean difference for a one-unit increase in fishpart. A one-unit increase can be N to M, M to MW, or MW to W. The three purple steps all equal beta 1. This is a strong assumption. Nothing in the data tells us that the categories are equally spaced.

22
00:04:56,703 --> 00:05:03,679
Pause the video and try it. Write each mean difference between neighbouring categories, for two different codes.

23
00:05:04,129 --> 00:05:16,588
With the code 0, 1, 2, 3, the means are beta 0, beta 0 plus beta 1, beta 0 plus 2 beta 1, and beta 0 plus 3 beta 1.

24
00:05:17,038 --> 00:05:20,387
Subtract neighbours. Each difference is beta 1.

25
00:05:20,837 --> 00:05:29,776
Now code N as 0, M as 10, MW as 11, and W as 15. These numbers are only labels too.

26
00:05:30,226 --> 00:05:37,394
The means are on the same kind of line. But the categories are now at 0, 10, 11, and 15.

27
00:05:37,844 --> 00:05:48,809
Subtract neighbours again. M minus N is 10 beta 1. MW minus M is beta 1. W minus MW is 4 beta 1.

28
00:05:49,259 --> 00:05:57,963
So the code alone changes the assumption about the mean differences. Each choice is equally strong, and the data did not choose it.

29
00:05:58,413 --> 00:06:18,339
We want a model with no assumption about the mean differences. Each category gets its own normal distribution. For category N, MeHg is normal with mean gamma N and variance sigma squared. The same holds for M, MW, and W, with the same sigma squared.

30
00:06:18,789 --> 00:06:28,133
The picture shows the four distributions. Each one has its own mean, gamma N, gamma M, gamma M W, and gamma W.

31
00:06:28,583 --> 00:06:43,836
To write this as a regression, we use the indicator function. I of A is 1 if the statement A is true, and 0 if it is false. The model has one indicator for each category, and each indicator has its own gamma.

32
00:06:44,286 --> 00:06:50,857
Put in each category. For a subject in N, only I of N is 1, so the mean is gamma N.

33
00:06:51,307 --> 00:06:56,107
For a subject in M, only I of M is 1, so the mean is gamma M.

34
00:06:56,557 --> 00:07:03,149
The same holds for MW and W. Their means are gamma M W and gamma W.

35
00:07:03,599 --> 00:07:14,820
So each gamma is the mean of one category. The model makes no assumption about the differences between the four means. The indicators are also called dummy variables.

36
00:07:15,270 --> 00:07:25,617
Here is another parameterization of the same model. It has an intercept beta 0, and indicators for M, MW, and W only.

37
00:07:26,067 --> 00:07:36,221
Put in each category. For N, all three indicators are 0, so the mean is beta 0. The purple dashed line is the level beta 0.

38
00:07:36,671 --> 00:07:41,919
For M, only I of M is 1. The mean is beta 0 plus beta M.

39
00:07:42,369 --> 00:07:51,116
For MW, the mean is beta 0 plus beta M W. For W, it is beta 0 plus beta W.

40
00:07:51,566 --> 00:08:08,014
So beta M is the mean of M minus the mean of N. beta M W and beta W are the differences from N in the same way. The arrows show them. This model also makes no assumption about the differences between categories.

41
00:08:08,464 --> 00:08:20,709
There is no indicator for N. N is the referent category, also called the baseline category. Its mean is beta 0. Every other beta is a difference from the baseline.

42
00:08:21,159 --> 00:08:27,069
Slide 19 asks: how are gamma and beta related? Pause the video and try it.

43
00:08:27,519 --> 00:08:39,295
Both parameterizations give the mean of each category. So set the means equal. For N: gamma N equals beta 0. The purple dashed line is this level.

44
00:08:39,745 --> 00:08:50,646
For M: gamma M equals beta 0 plus beta M. Subtract line 1. So beta M is gamma M minus gamma N. The blue arrow shows it.

45
00:08:51,096 --> 00:08:57,283
For MW, in the same way: beta M W is gamma M W minus gamma N.

46
00:08:57,733 --> 00:09:02,127
For W: beta W is gamma W minus gamma N.

47
00:09:02,577 --> 00:09:13,479
So both parameterizations have four parameters for the four category means. They describe the same model. We can always get one set of parameters from the other.

48
00:09:13,929 --> 00:09:23,529
Question type A asks you to write a model with a categorical covariate, and to interpret its parameters. Method card A has four steps.

49
00:09:23,979 --> 00:09:33,045
Step 1: pick the referent level, and list the other levels. In the picture, N is the referent. Its mean is the dashed line, beta 0.

50
00:09:33,495 --> 00:09:42,583
Step 2: write the model. It has the intercept beta 0, one indicator for each other level, and the other covariates, such as weight.

51
00:09:43,033 --> 00:09:48,175
Step 3: put in each level. You get a table with the mean of y for each level.

52
00:09:48,625 --> 00:10:04,219
Step 4: read each parameter from the table. beta 0 is the mean of the referent. The beta of a level is its mean minus the referent mean, at the same values of the other covariates. The arrows show these differences.

53
00:10:04,669 --> 00:10:18,792
Section 2 uses card A, now with weight in the model. Slide 20 asks for a model where mean MeHg is linear in weight for each level of fishpart, with a common slope but different intercepts.

54
00:10:19,242 --> 00:10:29,738
Step 2 of card A: write the model. In the first parameterization, each category has its own gamma, and gamma 1 is the coefficient of weight.

55
00:10:30,188 --> 00:10:40,428
In the second parameterization, beta 0 and the three betas give the levels, and beta 1 is the coefficient of weight. N is the referent.

56
00:10:40,878 --> 00:10:51,502
Here is the fit of the second model to the data. There is one line for each category. The lines are parallel, because all four groups share the slope of weight.

57
00:10:51,952 --> 00:11:07,995
Step 4: read the slope. The estimated common slope is 0.1511. So in each group, mean MeHg increases by 1.51 for each 10 kilograms of weight. The green step shows it.

58
00:11:08,445 --> 00:11:29,671
Interpret the first parameterization. Pause the video and try it. Step 3 of card A: the table of means. For a subject in N, the mean is gamma N plus gamma 1 times weight. For M, MW, and W, use gamma M, gamma M W, or gamma W in place of gamma N.

59
00:11:30,121 --> 00:11:45,929
Step 4: read each parameter. Put weight equal to 0. Then gamma N is the mean MeHg in group N at weight 0. The plot extends the lines to weight 0. The dots at the left are the four intercepts.

60
00:11:46,379 --> 00:12:03,830
The estimates are minus 9.730, minus 6.649, minus 7.491, and minus 6.357. Weight 0 is far outside the data, so these intercepts have no practical meaning.

61
00:12:04,280 --> 00:12:18,552
gamma 1 is the difference in mean MeHg for a one kilogram increase in weight, within one level x of fishpart. It is the same at every weight a, and in every group, because the lines are parallel.

62
00:12:19,002 --> 00:12:32,634
Now the second parameterization. Step 3 of card A: for N, the intercept is beta 0. For M, it is beta 0 plus beta M. The slope is beta 1 in every group.

63
00:12:33,084 --> 00:12:46,823
Step 4: beta 0 is the mean MeHg in group N at weight 0. beta 1 is the mean difference for a one kilogram increase in weight, in any group. It is the same as gamma 1.

64
00:12:47,273 --> 00:13:10,227
beta M is the difference in mean MeHg between group M and group N, at the same weight a. The lines are parallel, so this gap is the same at every weight. The arrows show beta M, beta M W, and beta W at three different weights. We say: beta M is the difference between M and N, adjusted for weight.

65
00:13:10,677 --> 00:13:28,405
The estimates are 3.081 for beta M, 2.239 for beta M W, and 3.374 for beta W. So, at the same weight, the groups that eat fish have higher mean MeHg than group N.

66
00:13:28,855 --> 00:13:39,138
Now the R example of Lecture 9, slide 43. First, the lecture fits the model with fishpart as a number, 0 to 3, and weight.

67
00:13:39,588 --> 00:14:03,332
The slide gives this coefficient table. Each row has the estimate, its standard error, the t value, and the two-sided p-value. The t value is the estimate over the standard error. The degrees of freedom are 135 minus 3, which is 132. The line under the table gives the range and the median of the residuals.

68
00:14:03,782 --> 00:14:23,537
The intercept is minus 10.43. It is the mean MeHg at weight 0 and fishpart 0, far outside the data. The weight slope is 0.1797 per kilogram, with t equal to 5.355 and a very small p-value.

69
00:14:23,987 --> 00:14:43,379
The fishpart estimate is 0.5406, with t equal to 1.690 and p equal to 0.0933. In the picture, this model gives four parallel lines with equal gaps of 0.5406.

70
00:14:43,829 --> 00:14:57,994
So this fit assumes that each step, N to M, M to MW, and MW to W, changes the mean by the same amount. The group means we saw earlier do not follow equal steps.

71
00:14:58,444 --> 00:15:08,001
The slide also shows the first rows of the design matrix X, from R. The first column is all 1s. It goes with the intercept.

72
00:15:08,451 --> 00:15:11,758
The second column holds the weight of each subject.

73
00:15:12,208 --> 00:15:22,960
The third column holds the fishpart code as a number. So one coefficient multiplies the code, and each step of one unit adds the same amount to the mean.

74
00:15:23,410 --> 00:15:35,527
Now the lecture recodes fishpart as a categorical variable. The table gives the count of each code: 10 none, 28 muscle, 88 muscle and whole, and 9 whole.

75
00:15:35,977 --> 00:15:42,356
R stores a categorical variable as a factor. A factor has a list of levels, in an order.

76
00:15:42,806 --> 00:16:00,065
This is step 1 of card A: the referent. R uses the first level as the referent. By default, R orders the levels alphabetically. The lecture code sets the order none, muscle, muscle whole, whole. Then the referent is none, N, as on the slides.

77
00:16:00,515 --> 00:16:24,643
Slide 46 fits both parameterizations in R. Mb is the model with an intercept. Its referent is N. The intercept is beta 0 hat, minus 9.730. The weight slope is 0.1511. The three fishpart rows are beta M hat, beta M W hat, and beta W hat.

78
00:16:25,093 --> 00:16:43,077
Mc is the model without an intercept. The minus 1 in the R formula removes it. Then R uses one indicator for each of the four levels. Its rows are the gamma hats. The weight slope is the same, 0.1511.

79
00:16:43,527 --> 00:17:02,364
Check the relation gamma equals beta 0 plus beta, with step 4 of card A. For M: minus 9.7302 plus 3.0815 is minus 6.6487. This is the fishpart muscle row of Mc.

80
00:17:02,814 --> 00:17:14,654
For MW: minus 9.7302 plus 2.2393 is minus 7.4909. This matches Mc.

81
00:17:15,104 --> 00:17:33,088
For W: minus 9.7302 plus 3.3736 is minus 6.3566. Mc gives minus 6.3565. The last digit differs only because we rounded the inputs.

82
00:17:33,538 --> 00:17:42,519
And gamma N hat equals beta 0 hat, minus 9.7302. So the two fits give the same lines.

83
00:17:42,969 --> 00:18:04,793
Slide 47 shows the design matrices of both models, for six subjects. This is step 2 of card A in matrix form. In Mb, the columns are the intercept, weight, and the indicators of M, MW, and W. Subject 102 is in group N, so all three indicators are 0.

84
00:18:05,243 --> 00:18:13,734
In Mc, the columns are weight and the four indicators. Now subject 102 has a 1 in the N column.

85
00:18:14,184 --> 00:18:27,667
So in Mc, each row has exactly one 1 among the indicators. In Mb, a row of the referent group N has no 1 among the indicators. Its mean comes from the intercept only.

86
00:18:28,117 --> 00:18:46,783
Slide 48 compares the fitted values of the two models. The R output gives the range of their differences: from about minus 2 times 10 to the minus 15, to about 1 times 10 to the minus 14. The plot shows the two fitted values of each subject.

87
00:18:47,233 --> 00:19:08,097
These differences are only computer rounding error. All points lie on the line Mc equals Mb. So the two parameterizations give the same fitted values, the same residuals, and the same sigma hat. Intervals for a mean response, or for a new observation, are also the same.

88
00:19:08,547 --> 00:19:28,579
Now a tutorial question of type A. Pause the video and try it. In Tutorial 3, question 1, the outcome is the average teacher salary of a school district. size coded is 1 for small, 2 for medium, and 3 for large districts. R fits salary on the factor of size.

89
00:19:29,029 --> 00:19:47,077
Part a asks for n. The output says 322 degrees of freedom. The residual degrees of freedom are n minus p plus 1, and the model has 3 coefficients. So n is 322 plus 3, which is 325.

90
00:19:47,527 --> 00:19:57,170
Part b. Step 1 of card A: the referent. R uses the first level, size 1, small districts. The other levels are 2 and 3.

91
00:19:57,620 --> 00:20:10,996
Step 2: the model. Salary i equals beta 0, plus beta 1 times the indicator of size 2, plus beta 2 times the indicator of size 3, plus epsilon i.

92
00:20:11,446 --> 00:20:26,849
Step 3: the table of means. Size 1 has mean beta 0. Size 2 has beta 0 plus beta 1. Size 3 has beta 0 plus beta 2. The purple marks are the three estimated means.

93
00:20:27,299 --> 00:20:39,032
Step 4: read each estimate. beta 0 hat, 31799.0, is the estimated mean salary in small districts.

94
00:20:39,482 --> 00:20:49,743
beta 1 hat, 3527.1, is the estimated difference in mean salary between medium and small districts.

95
00:20:50,193 --> 00:20:59,132
beta 2 hat, 6035.2, is the estimated difference in mean salary between large and small districts.

96
00:20:59,582 --> 00:21:11,550
Section 3 compares groups. Slide 25 says: both parameterizations make the same assumptions about the data. They only differ in their natural interpretation.

97
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:33,376
Lecture 9 asks whether mean MeHg varies by fishpart, with weight held constant. The null hypothesis says that the four groups have the same mean at each weight. In the first form, the four gammas are equal. In the second form, beta M, beta M W, and beta W are all 0.

98
00:21:33,826 --> 00:21:40,525
Under this null hypothesis, the four lines become one line. The gray dashed line shows it.

99
00:21:40,975 --> 00:21:55,396
The second form sets parameters equal to 0. This is simpler to test. So from here on, we use the model with an intercept. First we compare two groups. The test of all groups comes in section 4.

100
00:21:55,846 --> 00:22:08,710
Question type B asks about one number made from the parameters. It can be the difference between two group means, or the mean response at one x. Method card B has five steps.

101
00:22:09,160 --> 00:22:25,075
Step 1: write the quantity with the parameters. Call it theta. It is a sum of c j times beta j. In the picture, the red gap between M and MW at the same weight is beta M minus beta M W.

102
00:22:25,525 --> 00:22:29,514
Step 2: estimate it. Put the estimates into the same sum.

103
00:22:29,964 --> 00:22:48,076
Step 3: find its variance from sigma hat squared times V. R calls this matrix v cov. For a difference of two estimates, it is sigma hat squared times V j j plus V k k minus 2 V j k. The standard error is the square root.

104
00:22:48,526 --> 00:23:07,790
Step 4: for a test, t is the estimate minus the null value, over the standard error. It has a t distribution with n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom. For a confidence interval, take the estimate plus or minus the t quantile times the standard error.

105
00:23:08,240 --> 00:23:13,445
Step 5: conclude in words. Say that the comparison is at the same weight.

106
00:23:13,895 --> 00:23:28,765
Here is a type B question from slide 26. Pause the video and try it. Test whether the mean of M equals the mean of N, at the same weight a. Step 1 of card B: write the difference of the two means.

107
00:23:29,215 --> 00:23:38,516
Put in the model. Group M has beta 0 plus beta 1 a plus beta M. Group N has beta 0 plus beta 1 a.

108
00:23:38,966 --> 00:23:53,835
The beta 0 terms and the beta 1 a terms cancel. The difference is beta M: in the picture, the blue arrow from the N line up to the M line. So the null hypothesis is beta M equals 0.

109
00:23:54,285 --> 00:24:00,131
Step 2: the estimate. From the output, beta M hat is 3.0815.

110
00:24:00,581 --> 00:24:20,762
Step 3: the variance is sigma hat squared times V 3 3. The standard error is sigma hat times the square root of V 3 3: 2.5238 times the square root of 0.14267, which is 0.9533.

111
00:24:21,212 --> 00:24:48,348
Step 4: the observed t is 3.0815 over 0.9533, which is 3.233. The model has 5 parameters, so the degrees of freedom are 135 minus 5, which is 130. The red tails beyond 1.978 are the 5 percent rejection region. The two-sided p-value is 0.0016.

112
00:24:48,798 --> 00:25:02,814
Step 5: the p-value is less than 0.05, so we reject the null hypothesis. At the same weight, the mean MeHg of group M is different from the mean of group N.

113
00:25:03,264 --> 00:25:18,069
Another type B question, from slide 28. Pause the video and try it. Test whether the means of M and MW are equal, at the same weight. Neither group is the referent. Step 1: write the difference.

114
00:25:18,519 --> 00:25:29,698
Put in the model. M has beta 0 plus beta 1 a plus beta M. MW has beta 0 plus beta 1 a plus beta M W.

115
00:25:30,148 --> 00:25:48,196
beta 0 and beta 1 a cancel again. The difference is beta M minus beta M W. In the picture, it is the red gap between the blue and the teal lines. So the null hypothesis is beta M minus beta M W equal to 0.

116
00:25:48,646 --> 00:25:58,907
Step 2: the estimate is 3.0815 minus 2.2393, which is 0.8422.

117
00:25:59,357 --> 00:26:20,883
Step 3 needs the variance. beta M hat minus beta M W hat is a linear combination of the normal vector beta hat. So it is normal. Subtract the null value 0, and divide by its standard deviation. The result is standard normal. The next slide finds the variance of the difference.

118
00:26:21,333 --> 00:26:35,605
Step 3 of card B: the variance. The variance of a difference is the variance of the first, plus the variance of the second, minus 2 times their covariance. The green cells give the two variances.

119
00:26:36,055 --> 00:26:59,223
The variance matrix of beta hat is sigma squared times V. Number its rows from 1, in the order beta 0, beta 1, beta M, beta M W, beta W. So the variances are sigma squared V 3 3 and sigma squared V 4 4. The covariance is sigma squared V 3 4, the orange cells.

120
00:26:59,673 --> 00:27:24,291
Replace sigma squared by sigma hat squared, 6.3695. The V entries are 0.14267, 0.11199, and 0.10208. They give 0.05049. The 0.05049 uses the unrounded entries.

121
00:27:24,741 --> 00:27:34,533
So the variance is 0.3216, and the standard error is the square root, 0.5671.

122
00:27:34,983 --> 00:27:51,645
Step 4: the observed t is 0.8422 over 0.5671, which is 1.485. With 130 degrees of freedom, the two-sided p-value is 0.140.

123
00:27:52,095 --> 00:28:07,625
Step 5: the p-value is more than 0.05. So we do not reject the null hypothesis. The data give no evidence that groups M and MW differ in mean MeHg, at the same weight.

124
00:28:08,075 --> 00:28:22,582
Now Tutorial 3, question 1 d. Pause the video and try it. What is the precise interpretation of beta 2 minus beta 1? Give an estimate and a 95 percent confidence interval.

125
00:28:23,032 --> 00:28:41,592
Step 1 of card B: write the quantity. beta 2 minus beta 1 is the size 3 mean, beta 0 plus beta 2, minus the size 2 mean, beta 0 plus beta 1. So it is the difference in mean salary between large and medium districts.

126
00:28:42,042 --> 00:28:55,951
Step 2: the estimate is 6035.2 minus 3527.1, which is 2508.1. The orange arrow shows this gap.

127
00:28:56,401 --> 00:29:23,281
Step 3: R gives v cov, which is sigma hat squared times V. Add the two variances, 241977.18 and 136994.86, and subtract 2 times the covariance, 32012.55. The result is 314946.94.

128
00:29:23,731 --> 00:29:28,446
The standard error is the square root: 561.20.

129
00:29:28,896 --> 00:29:55,669
Step 4: the t quantile with 322 degrees of freedom is 1.967. The margin is 1.967 times 561.20, which is 1104.1. So the interval is 1404.0 to 3612.2. The green bar shows it, measured up from the size 2 mean.

130
00:29:56,119 --> 00:30:24,386
Step 5: beta 2 minus beta 1 is the difference in mean salary, large minus medium districts. The estimate is 2508.1, and the interval does not contain 0. The solution sheet uses unrounded numbers: 2508.041, and 1403.957 to 3612.125.

131
00:30:24,836 --> 00:30:43,311
Practice question 2 is step 3 of card B. The model has four coefficients, beta 1 to beta 4, and sigma is 10. The question gives X transpose X, and asks for the variance of beta 3 hat minus beta 4 hat. Pause the video and try it.

132
00:30:43,761 --> 00:31:01,851
Look at the zeros. X transpose X has two blocks on the diagonal: A for beta 1 and beta 2, and B for beta 3 and beta 4. The other entries are 0. By the hint, the inverse is block diagonal too, with A inverse and B inverse.

133
00:31:02,301 --> 00:31:16,509
The variance of the difference is sigma squared, times V 3 3 plus V 4 4 minus 2 V 3 4. These three entries are all in the B block of the inverse. So we only need B inverse.

134
00:31:16,959 --> 00:31:38,442
Invert the 2 by 2 block B. First the determinant: 384 times 543 is 208512. Minus 355 squared, 126025. The determinant is 82487.

135
00:31:38,892 --> 00:32:00,033
Swap the diagonal entries, change the sign of the off-diagonal entries, and divide by the determinant. The off-diagonal entry of B is minus 355, so the off-diagonal entry of B inverse is plus 355 over 82487.

136
00:32:00,483 --> 00:32:20,025
So V 3 3 is 543 over 82487. V 4 4 is 384 over 82487. V 3 4 is 355 over 82487.

137
00:32:20,475 --> 00:32:34,363
Now put them together. 543 plus 384 minus 2 times 355 is 217. Multiply by sigma squared, which is 100.

138
00:32:34,813 --> 00:32:49,469
Divide by 82487. The variance of beta 3 hat minus beta 4 hat is 0.2631. The positive covariance makes the variance of the difference smaller.

139
00:32:49,919 --> 00:33:14,303
Slide 48 uses Mb for intervals. A mean response is also one linear combination, so card B works. Step 1: the mean MeHg of subjects in W with weight 70 is x 0 transpose beta. x 0 is 1, 70, 0, 0, 1: the intercept, the weight, and the indicators of M, MW, and W.

140
00:33:14,753 --> 00:33:32,566
Step 2: mu 0 hat is minus 9.7302, plus 0.15105 times 70, plus 3.3736. This is 4.2172, the pink dot on the W line.

141
00:33:33,016 --> 00:33:47,501
Step 3: the standard error is sigma hat times the square root of x 0 transpose, X transpose X inverse, x 0. From the data, it is 0.8548.

142
00:33:47,951 --> 00:34:06,063
Step 4: the 95 percent confidence interval is 4.2172 plus or minus 1.978 times 0.8548. This is 2.526 to 5.908, the green bar.

143
00:34:06,513 --> 00:34:34,844
The same steps for group N with weight 60. The fit is minus 9.7302 plus 9.0632, which is minus 0.667. The standard error is 0.8733, and the interval is minus 2.394 to 1.061. MeHg cannot be negative. The negative values come from a straight line, outside most of the data of group N.

144
00:34:35,294 --> 00:34:48,691
Now a prediction interval for one new subject in W with weight 70. Add 1 under the square root, for the new error. The standard error becomes 2.6646.

145
00:34:49,141 --> 00:35:04,267
So the prediction interval is minus 1.054 to 9.488. The R output on the slide agrees. It is much wider than the confidence interval, because it includes the error of the new subject.

146
00:35:04,717 --> 00:35:17,730
Section 4 tests all groups at once. Slide 31 asks whether mean MeHg varies by fishpart, adjusted for weight. The parameter vector beta has five entries.

147
00:35:18,180 --> 00:35:35,908
Let beta star be the vector of beta M, beta M W, and beta W. Its length q is 3. We test the null hypothesis beta star equals 0, against the two-sided alternative, beta star not equal to 0.

148
00:35:36,358 --> 00:35:55,686
This null hypothesis is different from the ones of section 3. It does not set one parameter, or one linear combination, equal to a number. It sets three parameters to 0 together. This is a joint null hypothesis. A t test cannot do this. We need the F test.

149
00:35:56,136 --> 00:36:20,755
As in a t test, we measure how far the estimate is from 0, relative to its uncertainty. beta hat is normal. So the sub-vector beta star hat is normal too, with mean beta star, and variance sigma squared V star. V star is the sub-matrix of X transpose X inverse for the entries of beta star. It is the purple 3 by 3 block.

150
00:36:21,205 --> 00:36:34,474
Slide 32 defines the F statistic. It is beta star hat transpose, times V star inverse, times beta star hat. Divide this by q times sigma hat squared.

151
00:36:34,924 --> 00:36:45,505
Under the null hypothesis, F has an F distribution, with q and n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom. q is the length of beta star.

152
00:36:45,955 --> 00:37:01,145
With one parameter, this is the squared estimate over its estimated variance. With q parameters, V star inverse also accounts for the correlations between the estimates. Section 5 derives the distribution.

153
00:37:01,595 --> 00:37:20,496
Slide 40 computes the p-value as an upper tail probability, and asks why. Pause the video and try it. F is never negative. It is a quadratic form with a positive definite matrix, over a positive number. If beta star hat is close to 0, F is small.

154
00:37:20,946 --> 00:37:42,237
A large F means that beta star hat is far from 0, relative to its uncertainty. This is evidence against the null hypothesis. So the p-value is the probability of an F at least as large as the observed one: the upper tail. A small F is not evidence against the null hypothesis.

155
00:37:42,687 --> 00:37:53,801
At the 5 percent level with 3 and 130 degrees of freedom, the critical value is 2.674. The red area is the rejection region.

156
00:37:54,251 --> 00:38:04,833
Question type C asks whether several parameters are 0 together, for example whether all groups have the same mean. Method card C has five steps.

157
00:38:05,283 --> 00:38:13,411
Step 1: write the null hypothesis, beta star equals 0. q is the number of entries of beta star.

158
00:38:13,861 --> 00:38:25,530
Step 2: take the estimate beta star hat, and its variance block, sigma hat squared V star. In R, these are the rows and columns of v cov for beta star.

159
00:38:25,980 --> 00:38:37,095
Step 3: compute the observed F. It is beta star hat transpose, times the inverse of the variance block, times beta star hat, divided by q.

160
00:38:37,545 --> 00:38:50,750
Step 4: the p-value is the upper tail of the F distribution with q and n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom, beyond the observed F. The picture shows the curve and the tail.

161
00:38:51,200 --> 00:39:01,760
Step 5: conclude. Reject the null hypothesis if the p-value is less than alpha. Say that the result is adjusted for the other covariates.

162
00:39:02,210 --> 00:39:17,805
Slide 49 applies card C to the mercury data. Pause the video and try it. Step 1: the null hypothesis is beta star equals 0, with beta star the three fishpart parameters. So q is 3.

163
00:39:18,255 --> 00:39:38,436
Step 2: beta star hat is 3.0815, 2.2393, and 3.3736. The variance block is the fishpart block of the estimated variance matrix, sigma hat squared times V star. It already contains sigma hat squared.

164
00:39:38,886 --> 00:39:54,822
Step 3: multiply the inverse of this block by beta star hat. The computer solves this: 2.9401, minus 0.8407, and 1.4361.

165
00:39:55,272 --> 00:40:02,611
Then multiply by beta star hat transpose. Each entry of beta star hat times the matching entry.

166
00:40:03,061 --> 00:40:18,975
The three products are 9.060, minus 1.882, and 4.845. They add to 12.022. Divide by q, 3. The observed F is 4.007.

167
00:40:19,425 --> 00:40:44,961
Step 4: the p-value is the upper tail of the F distribution with 3 and 130 degrees of freedom, beyond 4.007. It is 0.009128. The slide prints 0.009127653. The red area beyond 2.674 is the 5 percent rejection region.

168
00:40:45,411 --> 00:40:59,726
Step 5: the p-value is less than 0.05. So we reject the null hypothesis. The data give evidence that mean MeHg varies by fishpart, adjusted for weight.

169
00:41:00,176 --> 00:41:19,845
Tutorial 3, question 1 c, is type C. Pause the video and try it. Does the size of the school district matter? Step 1 of card C: the null hypothesis is beta 1 equals beta 2 equals 0, so sizes 2 and 3 have the same mean as size 1. q is 2.

170
00:41:20,295 --> 00:41:33,223
Step 2: beta star hat is 3527.1 and 6035.2. The variance block is the size rows and columns of v cov.

171
00:41:33,673 --> 00:41:57,268
Step 3: invert the 2 by 2 block. First the determinant: 136994.86 times 241977.18, minus 32012.55 squared. This is 3.2125 times 10 to the 10.

172
00:41:57,718 --> 00:42:14,166
Swap the diagonal entries, change the sign of the off-diagonal entries, and divide by the determinant. Times beta star hat, this gives 0.020553 and 0.022222.

173
00:42:14,616 --> 00:42:40,024
Multiply by beta star hat transpose: 3527.1 times 0.020553 is 72.494, and 6035.2 times 0.022222 is 134.115. The sum is 206.609.

174
00:42:40,474 --> 00:42:45,231
Divide by q, 2. The observed F is 103.30.

175
00:42:45,681 --> 00:43:05,009
Step 4: the null distribution is F with 2 and 322 degrees of freedom. Its 5 percent critical value is 3.024. 103.3 is far beyond it. The p-value is 2.19 times 10 to the minus 35.

176
00:43:05,459 --> 00:43:21,395
Step 5: reject the null hypothesis. Mean salary differs among the district sizes. The R summary line shows the same F statistic: 103.3 on 2 and 322 degrees of freedom.

177
00:43:21,845 --> 00:43:43,456
Slide 42: these tests apply to any linear regression, not only to categorical covariates. Card B uses a t test for one number: one coefficient, like beta j equal to theta 0. Or one linear combination of coefficients, like beta M minus beta M W equal to 0.

178
00:43:43,906 --> 00:43:55,191
Card C uses an F test for a joint null hypothesis: several coefficients equal to 0 together, or a sub-vector beta star equal to 0.

179
00:43:55,641 --> 00:44:08,207
Both tests work for any covariates. When q is 1, the F test and the two-sided t test give the same answer. Practice question 1, in section 5, asks you to show this.

180
00:44:08,657 --> 00:44:21,094
Section 5 derives the distribution of F. Question type D asks for this derivation, or for the special case q equals 1. Method card D has four steps.

181
00:44:21,544 --> 00:44:24,680
Step 1: write the statistic with the estimates.

182
00:44:25,130 --> 00:44:41,983
Step 2: standardize. Write sigma squared V star as L times L transpose. Then z, L inverse times beta star hat minus beta star, is standard normal with variance I. For one number, Z is standard normal.

183
00:44:42,433 --> 00:44:53,420
Step 3: square and add. z transpose z is the sum of q squared standard normals: chi squared with q degrees of freedom. The purple box shows it.

184
00:44:53,870 --> 00:45:05,945
Step 4: divide by the independent chi squared variable from sigma hat squared. Divide each one by its degrees of freedom. The ratio has an F distribution.

185
00:45:06,395 --> 00:45:32,165
Step 2 of card D needs one fact from linear algebra: the Cholesky decomposition, slide 33. Any variance matrix V star can be written as L times L transpose. L is lower triangular: all entries above the diagonal are 0. Its diagonal entries are not negative. The left matrix is sigma hat squared V star for the mercury data.

186
00:45:32,615 --> 00:45:40,210
Here is its L, computed from the data. Multiply L by L transpose, and you get the left matrix back.

187
00:45:40,660 --> 00:45:58,281
When the matrix is positive-definite, all diagonal entries of L are positive. Here they are 0.953, 0.498, and 0.872. Then L has an inverse. We use L inverse in step 2.

188
00:45:58,731 --> 00:46:13,750
Step 1 of card D: the statistic. The blue cloud shows many possible values of beta star hat minus beta star, drawn for q equal to 2. The entries are correlated, and their spreads are not equal.

189
00:46:14,200 --> 00:46:29,987
Step 2: let L be the Cholesky factor of sigma squared V star. Define z as L inverse, times beta star hat minus beta star. Multiplying by L inverse turns the cloud into the round green cloud.

190
00:46:30,437 --> 00:46:42,533
Step 3 needs z transpose z. The transpose of a product is the product of the transposes, in the other order. So we get L inverse transpose, times L inverse.

191
00:46:42,983 --> 00:46:47,249
The transpose of L inverse is the inverse of L transpose.

192
00:46:47,699 --> 00:46:53,737
L transpose inverse, times L inverse, is the inverse of L L transpose.

193
00:46:54,187 --> 00:47:14,197
And L L transpose is sigma squared V star. So z transpose z is 1 over sigma squared, times the quadratic form with V star inverse. Under the null hypothesis, beta star is 0. Then this is the numerator of F over sigma squared, without the q.

194
00:47:14,647 --> 00:47:31,287
The slides ask: why is z N 0 I? Pause the video and try it. z is a linear map of a normal vector, so z is normal. Its mean is L inverse times the mean of beta star hat minus beta star, which is 0.

195
00:47:31,737 --> 00:47:38,329
Its variance is L inverse, times sigma squared V star, times L inverse transpose.

196
00:47:38,779 --> 00:47:42,321
Replace sigma squared V star by L L transpose.

197
00:47:42,771 --> 00:47:52,392
L inverse times L is I, and L transpose times its inverse is I. So the variance of z is the identity matrix I.

198
00:47:52,842 --> 00:48:02,442
A multivariate normal vector with variance I has independent entries. So z 1 to z q are independent standard normals.

199
00:48:02,892 --> 00:48:09,527
Step 3 of card D: square each one. Each square is chi squared with 1 degree of freedom.

200
00:48:09,977 --> 00:48:20,323
The sum of q independent chi squared 1 variables is chi squared with q degrees of freedom. The purple curve shows it for q equal to 3.

201
00:48:20,773 --> 00:48:42,725
So under the null hypothesis, the quadratic form over sigma squared is chi squared with q degrees of freedom. For step 4: from Lecture 7, n minus p plus 1, times sigma hat squared over sigma squared, is chi squared with n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom, independent of beta hat.

202
00:48:43,175 --> 00:49:00,263
Step 4 of card D builds F from two parts, as on slides 37 to 39. The first part, X 1, is the quadratic form over sigma squared. Under the null hypothesis, it is chi squared with q degrees of freedom.

203
00:49:00,713 --> 00:49:19,252
The second part, X 2, is n minus p plus 1, times sigma hat squared over sigma squared. It is chi squared with n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom. It is independent of X 1, because sigma hat squared is independent of beta hat.

204
00:49:19,702 --> 00:49:31,051
Recall from Lecture 1: divide each independent chi squared variable by its degrees of freedom, and take the ratio. The ratio has an F distribution.

205
00:49:31,501 --> 00:49:40,632
Put in X 1 and X 2. In the denominator, n minus p plus 1 cancels. The two marked sigma squared terms remain.

206
00:49:41,082 --> 00:49:48,741
They cancel too. What is left is the F statistic: the quadratic form over q times sigma hat squared.

207
00:49:49,191 --> 00:50:05,340
So under the null hypothesis, F has an F distribution with q and n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom. We can calculate it, because the unknown sigma is gone. Card C, step 4, uses this distribution.

208
00:50:05,790 --> 00:50:24,457
Practice question 1 is type D. It is also the exercise on page 41. Test beta j equal to 0 with the t statistic, and with the F statistic. Show that F equals t squared, and that the p-values are the same. Pause the video and try it.

209
00:50:24,907 --> 00:50:43,552
Step 1 of card D: write both statistics. Method 1: the t statistic is beta j hat, over sigma hat times the square root of V j j. Under the null hypothesis, it has a t distribution with n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom.

210
00:50:44,002 --> 00:50:53,111
Method 2: let beta star be beta j alone. Then q is 1, and V star is the 1 by 1 matrix V j j.

211
00:50:53,561 --> 00:51:05,700
Put these into the F statistic. The inverse of a 1 by 1 matrix is 1 over its entry. So F is beta j hat squared, over sigma hat squared V j j.

212
00:51:06,150 --> 00:51:12,166
Write it as a square. The inside is the t statistic. So F equals t squared.

213
00:51:12,616 --> 00:51:26,483
Check with the data. For weight in Mb, t is 4.430, and t squared is 19.62. The F statistic for beta 1 alone is also 19.62.

214
00:51:26,933 --> 00:51:39,135
Now the p-values. The t test uses the t distribution with nu degrees of freedom. Here nu is n minus p plus 1, which is 130 in the example.

215
00:51:39,585 --> 00:51:50,700
The two-sided p-value is the probability that the absolute value of T is at least the absolute observed t. This is the area of the two blue tails.

216
00:51:51,150 --> 00:52:16,089
Steps 2 to 4 of card D, for one number. Recall from Lecture 1: T is Z over the square root of X over nu. Square it. T squared is Z squared over 1, divided by X over nu. Z squared is chi squared with 1 degree of freedom, and it is independent of X. So T squared has an F distribution with 1 and nu degrees of freedom.

217
00:52:16,539 --> 00:52:42,331
The F test p-value is the probability that F is at least t observed squared. This is the probability that T squared is at least t observed squared. That is the same as the absolute value of T at least the absolute observed t. So the two p-values are equal. The critical values agree too: 3.914 is 1.978 squared, up to rounding.

218
00:52:42,781 --> 00:53:00,637
To recap. Card A: a numeric code forces fixed mean differences, so use indicators. Pick the referent, write beta 0 plus one indicator for each other level, make the table of means, and read each beta as a difference from the referent, at the same weight.

219
00:53:01,087 --> 00:53:11,497
Card B: for one linear combination, write theta, estimate it, find the variance with the covariance term, and use a t test or a confidence interval.

220
00:53:11,947 --> 00:53:26,241
Card C: for a joint null hypothesis, compute F from beta star hat and its variance block, and use the upper tail of the F distribution with q and n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom.

221
00:53:26,691 --> 00:53:46,595
Card D derives this distribution: standardize with L inverse, square and add to get chi squared, and divide by the independent chi squared from sigma hat squared. With q equal to 1, F is t squared. The next video adds interactions: lines with different slopes.
